From: Timur Ravilov <93673987+t0b1kent@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:24:03 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] EXPERIMENT: MACRUNNER_FEX_HOOK_GUARD - Flush and Dirty hooks
 (HG3, on top of HG2)

Third door of the same self-deadlock as HG1/HG2. Measured 30.09 11:13 (Stardew 1.6.15, .NET 6 W^X=1, alias layer on, HG1+HG2 gate on, span recorder):
the main thread holds CodeInvalidationMutex EXCLUSIVELY in InvalidateIntervalInternal (JitChanged -> INVALIDATE -> Request -> InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange
-> SharedScan = LookupCache::InvalidateRange), Erase() runs the block delinkers (JIT.cpp DirectBlockDelinker/IndirectBlockDelinker), they write one word into
the code buffer and ClearICache; on Windows that is FlushInstructionCache -> NtFlushInstructionCache, and the ARM64EC/WOW64 flush hook
(FlushInstructionCacheHeavy / BTCpu64FlushInstructionCache / BTCpuFlushInstructionCache2 / ...Heavy) takes ThreadCreationMutex and calls
InvalidateAlignedInterval on a page of FEX's OWN code buffer, which asks for the same exclusive mutex: LOCK_WAIT id 13 under the thread's own lock.
HG1/HG2 suppressed Free/Alloc/Protect/Map/Unmap only, so the gate was on and the counters were 0.

Kind::Flush and Kind::Dirty join the suppressed hooks (NotifyMemoryDirty is the same shape: tracker call under ThreadCreationMutex). Inside a guarded FEX section only
FEX code runs, guest memory is neither flushed nor dirtied there, and the code buffer is not tracked: nothing to invalidate. Same gate (default off, behaviour unchanged),
same TLS depth, counters hs_flush / hs_dirty in the macrunner-fex-jit line, hook_suppressed sums all seven kinds.
NOT covered (no evidence, not touched): BTCpu64NotifyReadFile takes CodeInvalidationMutex exclusively in its Before hook.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
